// The ontology agent, one turn at a time, and the graph builds of approved versions.
//
// The messages carry the same JSON the `ontology-agent turn` CLI reads and writes, so the CLI
// and this service share one contract. Its JSON Schemas are in `schema/`:
// `turn_request.json`, `event.json` and `turn_result.json` for a turn,
// `ingest_{request,event,result}.json`, `extract_{request,event,result}.json` and
// `delete_load_{request,event,result}.json` for a graph build, `clues_{request,event}.json`
// for People enrichment's Clues, `read_{request,result}.json` for a read,
// `compare_{request,result}.json` for a comparison of two loads,
// `ask_{request,event,result}.json` for a question, `title_{request,result}.json` for a
// title, `probe_{request,result}.json` for a probe, `catalog.json` for the models Helix prices,
// and `schema/VERSION` holds the contract version they describe.
//
// Turn, Ask, Extract, Title and Probe run on the models their request names (`models`, or a
// probe's `endpoint` and `checks`). The endpoint's API key never goes in the JSON: it travels
// in the call's `x-foundry-model-key` metadata, which the server takes out before it reads the
// request. A provider that needs a key, called without one, is INVALID_ARGUMENT, and so is a
// key that isn't 1 to 512 printable ASCII characters; a server replaying its response store
// needs none and ignores one. A provider kind this build doesn't call (GetInfo's `providers`
// lists those it does) is FAILED_PRECONDITION `provider_not_supported`, and so is a model
// priced from Helix's table that the table doesn't hold.
//
// The server takes and sends messages of up to 32 MiB; a client must raise its own limit to
// match (gRPC's default is 4 MiB), since a result carries the session state (up to 1 MiB),
// the draft and any approved bundle.

